### Lens Agents

**For:** Platform, security, and IT teams putting AI agents into production.

A governed platform for running AI agents against enterprise systems — any agent, any model, any environment, under your policy.

- Each agent gets its own identity instead of borrowing a person's credentials.
- Policy-controlled access decides which systems, tools, and network destinations an agent may reach.
- Credentials are substituted at the network boundary; the agent holds only a placeholder, so an agent that leaks everything it can read leaks placeholders.
- Intelligent PII masking and rehydration: sensitive values are masked before a prompt leaves your environment and restored in the response, so inference providers only ever see placeholders while agents still work with real context.
- Every action is logged and auditable, and denials are recorded too.
- Helping organizations meet EU AI Act obligations is a focus of the product. Attributable agent identity, a complete record of actions, and declared autonomy levels supply the controls and evidence Article 50 transparency calls for. The platform provides the building blocks; compliance remains something the organization achieves, not something a product confers.
- Each agent runs in its own isolated execution environment, so a compromised agent is contained.
- Runs three kinds of agent: MCP clients your teams already use (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, your CI), keeping their current workflow; your own agents supplied as a container image and run in a sandbox; and long-running agents configured in place, such as Lens Prism, OpenClaw, or Hermes Agent.
- Autonomous agents get the same hard guardrails as everything else — their own identity, policy-scoped access, masked data, isolated execution, and a full audit trail. An agent that decides its own next step is still bounded by what you allowed it to reach.
- Single sign-on is OIDC — Okta, Entra ID, and JumpCloud are validated, and any OIDC provider works. SAML and SCIM are not supported.
- Installed with Helm into your own Kubernetes cluster: a public cloud, a private cloud, or an air-gapped data center with no external connectivity. The server, sandboxes, database, and audit trail all run there, so data residency is where you installed it.

